SYSCALL REFERENCE: PROCESS, TIME, TERMINAL

PicoNIX is educational and only partially POSIX-like. User code
must use actual PicoNIX wrappers; do not assume full exec(),
wait groups, signals, fork semantics or Linux /proc/pid content.

getpid()
  Query current process PID. Useful for trace correlation.

exit / _exit
  Terminate current task; release FD/resources through kernel.
  On-device 'cc' supports return from main and a narrow builtin
  list; do not assume libc exit() callable from cc C source.

yield()
  Voluntarily let scheduler run another ready task. It is not
  a substitute for select() when waiting on multiple sockets.

msleep(milliseconds)
  Sleep/schedule without busy-looping. Do not sleep for long in
  commands run through MCP (~3 s timeout).

fork/spawn/waitpid
  The shell and trace start child programs using PicoNIX's own
  process APIs. Check userland wrappers for signature and behavior
  before writing a native app. Task slots are limited to six.

TERMINAL MODE
  stty raw
  stty cooked
  tty
The terminal has its own line-editing and echo behavior.
Interactive programs should use a terminal, not MCP pipes.

OBSERVE, THEN ACT
  ps
  lsof [PID]
  trace show [PID]
  kill PID  # only if explicitly authorized; may kill active server

C COMPILER WARNING
  The on-device cc is NOT a general C compiler for these APIs.
  Use native, host-built user programs for full syscall wrappers.
  See /man/languages/c.
